CHAPTERS = [
dict(title="The Lighthouse Keepers", scene="headland", paras=[
"Hello, and welcome back to The Quiet Archive. Tonight we are going to spend a long, calm night with the people who kept the lights burning along the coast, about a hundred and sixty years ago. Nothing much will happen. A lamp will be lit, a lens will turn, a kettle will boil, and the sea will go on breathing against the rocks. You are welcome to drift off whenever you like.",
"Before we begin, let your shoulders drop a little lower. Let your hands rest, heavy and still. Breathe in slowly through your nose, as if you were smelling cold, clean air off the sea. And let it go again, all the way out, the way a wave slides back down a shingle beach.",
"We are on the far north coast of Scotland, in the eighteen sixties, on a headland of dark rock high above the water. A white tower stands at the very edge, with a cluster of low cottages beside it. The sun has just gone down behind the hills, and a long band of pale green light still lingers over the sea.",
"For the rest of tonight, we will keep the watch together. We will climb the tower, light the lamp, sit beside the great glass lens, and look out across the dark water at the passing ships. And if you happen to fall asleep before morning, that is perfectly all right. The light will keep shining without you.",
]),

dict(title="The Cottages Below the Tower", scene="headland", paras=[
"The keepers' cottages are built of stone, thick and low against the wind, with slate roofs held down by heavy flags along the eaves. Their walls are whitewashed every summer, so that sailors can pick out the station by day as well as by night. A short path of crushed shells and gravel runs between the cottage doors and the base of the tower.",
"Beyond the wall, the headland slopes away in short, springy turf, cropped close by rabbits and sheep. Clumps of sea pinks grow along the cliff edge, their pale flowers closed now for the night. Below, on a broad ledge of rock, a colony of seabirds has settled down to sleep, shuffling and murmuring softly, and then falling quiet.",
"Around the buildings runs a high stone wall. Inside it, sheltered from the salt breeze, there is a small garden. Rows of potatoes and kale grow in the dark soil, and a few hardy cabbages, and a bed of rhubarb under an upturned bucket. In the corner stands a tidy pile of peat, cut from the moor and stacked to dry, ready for the kitchen fires.",
"Two families live here. The principal keeper has the larger cottage, nearest the tower, and his assistant has the smaller one beside it. In the eighteen sixties, a keeper here works for the Northern Lighthouse Board, which looks after every light around the coast of Scotland. He wears a dark uniform jacket with brass buttons, and he is expected to keep his station spotless.",
"In the principal keeper's kitchen, the evening meal is over. His wife is wiping the table with a damp cloth, and the smell of oatcakes and peat smoke hangs in the warm air. A kettle sits at the back of the range, murmuring quietly to itself. On the windowsill, a row of geraniums leans towards the last of the light.",
"Their children have been sent to wash their faces. There are three of them, and they go to a little school a few miles down the road, walking there in the mornings with their satchels and their oatcake pieces. In the long summer evenings they collect gulls' feathers and smooth stones from the shore. Tonight they are tired, and their beds are already turned down.",
"Out in the byre, a single cow is chewing slowly, and a few hens have settled on their perch in a row, feathers puffed against the cool. The keepers are allowed a patch of grazing on the headland, and most stations keep a cow for milk and a few hens for eggs. The nearest shop is a long walk away, so a lighthouse family learns to look after itself.",
"There is a sundial on a stone pillar in the garden, and a tall flagstaff by the wall. Inside the principal keeper's cottage, a good clock ticks on the mantelpiece. The keepers are careful about time. The lamp must be lit at sunset, exactly, and put out at sunrise, exactly, and every one of those minutes is written down.",
"On washing day, the keepers' wives hang the sheets out on a line between two posts, and the wind off the sea snaps them dry in an hour. Tonight the line is empty, swinging a little in the breeze. A pair of small boots stands upside down on the doorstep, drying, and a fishing rod leans against the wall beside them.",
"Now the green glow over the sea is fading into blue, and the first stars are coming out above the tower. The principal keeper looks up at the sky, then at the clock. He takes his jacket from the hook behind the door, buttons it neatly, and steps out into the cool evening air. It is almost time.",
]),

dict(title="Lighting-Up Time", scene="watchroom", paras=[
"The door at the foot of the tower is heavy and painted a deep green. Inside, it is cool and quiet, and smells of stone and paint and lamp oil. A spiral stair of stone steps winds up and up around the inside of the wall, each step worn a little smooth in the middle by years of patient feet.",
"Follow the keeper as he climbs. His boots ring softly on the steps, and his hand slides along the polished brass rail. Here and there a small window lets in a square of fading blue, and you glimpse the sea far below, grey and wrinkled and calm. Round and round you go, a little higher with every turn, a little further from the ground.",
"Partway up, you pass the oil room. Rows of large metal cans stand along the wall, filled with colza oil, pressed from the seeds of a yellow-flowered plant, a cousin of the cabbage and the turnip. It burns with a clear, steady flame and very little smoke. In a few years, many lights will change to paraffin, which is brighter and cheaper. But tonight, it is colza.",
"Near the top of the stair is the service room, a small round room just beneath the lantern. Here the keeper keeps his tools, his spare wicks, his soft cloths, his brushes, and a tall clock. A narrow iron ladder leads up through a hatch in the ceiling into the lantern itself, the glass room at the very top of the tower.",
"Everything here is kept gleaming. The brass handrail, the door handles, the oil cans, the little brass plates on the clock. Polishing is part of every keeper's day, and every room is cleaned to a strict routine. Now and then, without warning, a superintendent arrives on an inspection, and runs a white glove along the top of a doorframe, and expects to find no dust at all.",
"He climbs the ladder and steps up into the lantern. All around him are tall windows of thick glass, set in a frame of bronze, and through them the whole sky is turning deep blue. At the centre of the room stands the lens, taller than a man, covered all day long by a linen cloth to keep off the dust and the sun.",
"During the day, the keepers draw curtains around the inside of the lantern. A great lens can gather the sunlight just as easily as it gathers lamplight, and if the curtains were left open, the summer sun could scorch the brass and crack the delicate wicks. So every morning the curtains are closed, and every evening, at lighting-up time, they are drawn back again.",
"The keeper lifts the cloth from the lens and folds it neatly over his arm. He checks the clock, and the sky, and the clock again. Then he opens a small glass door in the side of the lens, reaches in with a lighted taper, and touches it to the wick. A small blue flame runs around the ring, and turns to gold.",
"He waits, watching the flame. Slowly he turns the little brass wheel that raises the wick, a fraction at a time, until the flame stands tall and even and does not smoke. Then he closes the glass door. Outside, far across the water, a fisherman on his way home looks up and sees the light come on, and knows exactly where he is.",
"The keeper writes the time in a small notebook, ready to copy into the log. Then he steps back and looks at the lens, glowing softly now from within, as if it were full of warm honey. Through the windows, the sky is a deep, clear blue, and the sea has turned to ink, and the first beam of the night is reaching out across it.",
]),

dict(title="The Great Lens", scene="lantern", paras=[
"The lamp itself is not very large. If you took it out of the lens and set it on a kitchen table, it would light a room, and not much more. It has several round wicks, one inside another, like the rings of a tree, so that a broad, bright flame rises from the centre. But on its own, it could never be seen twenty miles out at sea.",
"What makes the difference is the glass. Around the lamp stands a tall cage of brass, and fixed in the cage are hundreds of curved pieces of glass, ground and polished by hand. Some sit in rings above the flame, and some below, and some in a broad belt around the middle. Together they look like a great glass beehive, or a lantern made of ice.",
"The idea came from a quiet French engineer named Augustin Fresnel, in the eighteen twenties. Instead of one enormous, heavy lens, he built his out of many thin rings and prisms, each one shaped to catch a little of the lamplight and bend it, gently, into a single direction. Light that would have wandered off into the sky or down into the ground is gathered up and sent out across the sea.",
"Stand beside it now, and look closely. The glass is so clear you can hardly see it, except where the edges of the prisms catch the flame. Every ring holds a tiny golden image of the lamp, and every image glows. Tiny rainbows tremble along the edges of the glass, violet and green and gold, and slide away as you move your head.",
"The lens is very slowly turning. It rests on a bed of rollers, and beneath it, in the service room, a clockwork machine of brass wheels is pushing it gently round. The clockwork is driven by a heavy weight, hanging on a cable inside the tower. As the weight sinks, the wheels turn, and the great lens revolves, steady and silent.",
"Every few hours, the weight reaches the bottom of its fall, and the keeper must wind it up again. He fits a crank handle to the machine and turns it, round and round, with a slow, patient rhythm, and a soft clicking of the ratchet. Then the weight is raised, and the lens can turn for another few hours without him.",
"In the middle of the lens there are broad round panels of glass, like great glass eyes. As each one swings past, the light gathers into a single bright beam that sweeps across the water. Out at sea, a sailor sees a sudden, steady flash, and then darkness, and then another flash, all in a pattern that belongs to this light and no other.",
"Inside the lantern, the turning lens throws slow patterns of light across the walls and the ceiling, bands of gold that drift past and fade and come round again. If you stand still and watch them for a while, they are almost hypnotic, like the light from a fire, or the reflections of water on the underside of a bridge.",
"Every lighthouse has its own character, as the keepers call it. One might show a single white flash every half a minute. Another, two flashes close together. Another, a light that slowly grows and fades. Another, red and white in turn. A ship's captain with his book of lights can count the seconds between the flashes, and know, even on the darkest night, which headland he is passing.",
]),

dict(title="The First Watch", scene="watchroom", paras=[
"Now the light is burning, and the long night watch begins. The keeper settles at a small desk in the service room, beneath the lantern, where he can hear the soft tick of the clockwork above his head and see the glow of the lamp through the open hatch. The watches are shared between the keepers, so that one man is always awake while the light is shining.",
"On the desk lies the logbook, a heavy book bound in dark cloth, with ruled columns on every page. In neat, careful handwriting, the keeper writes the date, and the time he lit the lamp. Then the direction of the wind, the state of the sea, the weather. Fine and clear, he writes. Light breeze from the west. Sea smooth.",
"Every so often, he climbs back up into the lantern to check the flame. Over the hours, the wicks slowly char at the top, and the flame begins to droop, or to smoke a little at one side. So he trims them, carefully, with a small pair of scissors, cutting away the blackened edge until the flame stands bright and even again. In America, keepers were sometimes nicknamed wickies, for exactly this reason.",
"Between these small tasks, there is time to sit. The keeper fills his pipe from a tin on the desk, and lights it, and leans back in his wooden chair. Through the little window beside him, he can see the beam of the lighthouse sweeping out across the water, catching the tops of the waves for a moment, and moving on.",
"On the stove in the corner, a small kettle is warming. When it starts to sing, he pours the water into a brown teapot, and waits, and then fills his mug. He drinks it slowly, with both hands around the mug, looking out at the dark. The tea tastes of smoke and milk and a little sugar, and it is very good.",
"Sometimes, on these quiet watches, a keeper reads. A book of sermons, perhaps, or a volume of travels, or a newspaper that came with the last delivery and is already two weeks old. He reads by the small oil lamp on the desk, a page at a time, looking up at the end of each page to check the light.",
"The tower makes its own small sounds at night. The tick of the clock. The hum of the wind across the gallery rail outside. The soft rush of the lamp's flame. Now and then a creak from somewhere in the stonework as it cools. The keeper knows every one of these sounds, and they tell him that all is well.",
"Moths sometimes find their way up to the lantern on summer nights, drawn by the glow, and flutter softly against the outside of the glass. Tonight it is too cool for moths. Instead, a small brown bird has tucked itself into a corner of the gallery outside, out of the wind, with its head beneath its wing, fast asleep.",
"Below, in the cottages, the lights have gone out one by one. The assistant keeper is asleep, because he will take the middle watch, from midnight until four in the morning. His wife is asleep beside him, and the children are asleep in the little room under the roof, and the cow in the byre has folded her legs beneath her and closed her eyes.",
"The keeper writes another line in the log. Ten o'clock. Light burning well. Wind westerly, light. Then he puts down the pen, wipes it on a scrap of cloth, and sits back to watch the slow beam turning, turning, out over the quiet sea.",
]),

dict(title="A Tower in the Sea", scene="rocktower", paras=[
"Far out on the horizon, beyond the headland, another light is flashing. It belongs to a different kind of lighthouse altogether, a rock station, built on a reef many miles from land. There is no garden there, no cottage, and no cow. There is only the tower, and the sea all around it.",
"Some of these towers are among the great works of their age. The Bell Rock, off the east coast of Scotland, rises from a reef that disappears beneath the water at every high tide. It was built by Robert Stevenson and his men between eighteen hundred and seven and eighteen hundred and ten, stone by stone, in the few hours each day when the rock stood clear of the sea.",
"Off the west coast, beyond the island of Tiree, stands Skerryvore, designed by his son Alan and first lit in eighteen forty four. It is a tall, slender tower of granite, curving gently outward at the base like the trunk of a great tree, so that the waves slide up around it and fall away. It is the tallest lighthouse in Scotland.",
"Inside a rock tower, everything is stacked, one room on top of the next. At the bottom, water tanks and coal. Above that, the oil store. Then a store room for food and rope. Then a kitchen, with a small stove and a table. Then a bedroom. Then the service room, and at the very top, the lantern. Each room is round, and joined to the next by a ladder or a narrow stair.",
"The walls at the base of a rock tower are enormously thick, made of great blocks of stone cut to fit together like the pieces of a puzzle, each one locked to its neighbours above and below and on either side. Higher up, the walls grow thinner, and the rooms grow a little wider. The whole tower is shaped to stand quietly, year after year, while the sea comes and goes around it.",
"Because the walls are round, the beds are curved. They are built into the wall like long wooden boxes, bending gently to follow the stone, each with a curtain across the front. Keepers have called them banana bunks. A man lying in one at night can hear the sea moving around the foot of the tower, a long way below, like slow breathing.",
"Three keepers live on a rock station at a time, with a fourth ashore having his turn of rest. Every few weeks, if the weather allows, a small steamer comes out from the shore station, bringing fresh food, water, letters, and a relief keeper. One man climbs down the ladder into the boat to go home to his family, and another climbs up to take his place.",
"Ashore, the families of the rock keepers live together at a shore station, in a row of cottages near the harbour. At some stations, a signal tower stands on the shore, so that messages can be passed back and forth, by flags or by signals raised on a mast, between the families on land and the men out on the rock.",
"Each man takes his turn at the cooking. They bake their own bread in the little oven, and the smell of it rises up through the rooms of the tower, warm and comforting. In the evenings there are games of draughts and cribbage at the kitchen table, and books from a wooden library box, which is changed for a new box of books at each relief.",
"Some keepers make things to pass the hours. Rag rugs, hooked from scraps of old cloth. Little models of ships, rigged with fine thread. Carved boxes and picture frames. On calm days, some of them fish from the gallery, letting a long line down into the green water far below. And all the while, the light turns above their heads.",
]),

dict(title="Passing Ships", scene="rocktower", paras=[
"Out on the water, a small sailing ship is passing, a little schooner with two masts, heading south along the coast. Her sails are pale grey in the starlight. At her stern, a single lantern glows, and on either side of her bow there is a coloured light, red on the left side and green on the right, so that other ships can tell which way she is going.",
"On her deck, a sailor stands at the wheel, his collar turned up against the cool air. He has been watching the lighthouse for the last hour, counting the flashes, checking them against the book of lights in the little cabin. He knows exactly where he is now. The rocks are well to the west of him, and the way ahead is clear.",
"For him, the beam sweeping across the water is a kind of company. Out here, with only the creak of the rigging and the wash of the bow, it is good to know that someone on that dark headland is awake, and keeping the lamp trimmed, and watching. He raises a hand towards the light, though no one can possibly see it.",
"In the lantern, the keeper sees the schooner too, a tiny cluster of lights moving slowly across the black water. He watches it for a long time. He does not know her name, or where she is going, or what she carries. Perhaps coal, or timber, or barrels of salted herring. It does not matter. She is passing safely, and that is the whole point of the light.",
"On a clear night like this, the keepers see many ships go by. Fishing boats heading out for the night, with their nets piled in the stern. Coastal steamers with a plume of smoke and a row of small lit portholes. Now and then, a great sailing ship from far away, her sails stacked high like clouds, gliding past on her way to a distant port.",
"Some of those ships have been at sea for months. Their crews have watched the stars and the compass and the colour of the water. And then, one evening, low on the horizon, a tiny light appears, flashing in a pattern they recognise from the book. It is the first sign of home, and the men gather at the rail to watch it grow.",
"Over the years, the keepers come to know the coast as well as their own kitchens. Every rock and current and headland. Every light along the shore, flashing in its own rhythm, one here, another there, far off along the coast, all keeping the same quiet watch. On some nights, from the top of a tall tower, a keeper can see three or four other lighthouses at once, like distant friends.",
"The keepers know the sea birds too. The gannets, folding their wings and diving like arrows into the water. The puffins on the cliffs in early summer, with their bright striped beaks. The eider ducks rocking in the bay. And on autumn nights, flocks of small birds on their long journey south, which sometimes rest for a little while on the gallery rail before flying on.",
"The schooner is further off now, her stern lantern a small gold point on the dark water. The swell lifts her gently, and lets her down, and lifts her again. Soon she will round the next headland, and the next light will take up the watch, and guide her on through the night towards the morning.",
"The keeper writes in the log. Eleven o'clock. One schooner passing southward. Then he looks up again, through the tall glass windows, at the beam sweeping slowly out across the empty sea, and in again, and out again, as steady as a heartbeat.",
]),

dict(title="The Middle Watch", scene="lantern", paras=[
"Just before midnight, there is a soft knock on the door at the foot of the tower. The assistant keeper has come to take the middle watch. He climbs the long spiral stair, yawning, his lamp in his hand, and comes up into the service room with the cool night air still clinging to his coat.",
"The two men speak quietly for a few minutes. The light is burning well. The clockwork has been wound at eleven. The wind is light and steady from the west. A schooner went by an hour ago. Then the first keeper signs the log, puts on his cap, and goes down the stair, round and round, towards his warm bed in the cottage below.",
"Now the assistant keeper is alone in the tower. He climbs up into the lantern and stands beside the great lens for a while, warming his hands near the glass. Close to the lamp, the air is warm and smells faintly of hot oil. A little further away, near the windows, it is cool, and the glass is beaded with a fine mist of condensation.",
"He takes a soft chamois leather from his pocket and walks slowly round the inside of the lantern, wiping each pane of glass in turn, so that nothing will dim the light. Through each pane, as it clears, the night appears again, the dark water, the pale line of foam along the rocks, the stars crowding over the sea.",
"Every morning, after the light is put out, the keepers will clean the lens itself, with soft linen and a little spirit, prism by prism, ring by ring, without a single scratch. It can take an hour or more. A keeper polishes the same glass every day of his working life, and is quietly proud that it shines.",
"Then he steps out through a small door onto the gallery, the narrow iron balcony that runs right around the outside of the lantern. The air is cold and fresh and smells of salt and seaweed. Far below, the waves are folding quietly against the foot of the cliff, a long, slow hush, and then a pause, and then another hush.",
"Above him, the sky is enormous. With no towns nearby, the stars are thick and bright, and the pale river of the Milky Way runs right across the sky from one side to the other. Behind him, the beam swings out over his head, a broad pale arm of light reaching far across the water, and slowly turns away again.",
"Low in the north, there is a faint, pale glow above the horizon, soft and shifting, perhaps the first stirring of the northern lights. It brightens a little, and fades, and brightens again, and the keeper watches it with quiet pleasure. He has seen the lights many times from this gallery, curtains of green and rose moving silently over the sea, and they still make him stop and stare.",
"He stays out there for a little while, his hands resting on the cold rail, breathing the sea air, watching the beam go round. This is the part of the job many keepers love most, though few of them would say so. The quiet. The stars. The feeling of being awake and useful while the rest of the world sleeps.",
"Then he goes back inside, closes the little door behind him, and sits down at the desk. He winds the clockwork, slowly, round and round, with that soft clicking of the ratchet. He trims the wick. He writes in the log. One o'clock. All well. And outside, the light goes on turning.",
]),

dict(title="All Is Well", scene="headland", paras=[
"Down in the cottages, everyone is sleeping now. The principal keeper lies on his back with his hands folded, breathing slowly. His wife has pulled the quilt up to her chin. The children are curled up together under the roof, and the peat fire in the kitchen has sunk to a soft red glow beneath its ashes.",
"Far out at sea, on the rock station, the keepers are sleeping too, in their curved wooden bunks, with the curtains drawn, and the slow sound of the sea around the foot of the tower. Only one man in each tower is awake, sitting quietly beneath the lantern, with a mug of tea going cold beside him and the log open on the desk.",
"Let yourself rest now, like the families in the cottages, warm and safe while someone else keeps watch. You do not need to count the flashes. You do not need to trim the wick, or wind the clock, or write in the log. All of that is taken care of. All you need to do is breathe, slowly, and let the light go round.",
"In, as the beam swings out across the water. Out, as it turns slowly away. In, and out. The sea breathes against the rocks below, and the stars turn slowly overhead, and the great lens turns with them, patient and steady, all through the long night, until the sky grows pale in the east.",
"Thank you for keeping the watch with The Quiet Archive tonight. The lamp is lit, the lens is turning, and the ships are passing safely by. Sleep well. Goodnight.",
]),
]
